    "body": "A planned master’s degree in mining and mineral processing would take 12 to 16 months. Credit: The Northern Miner\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 16, 2026\nMemorial University is developing a shorter route to training mining professionals as the industry faces a wait of almost six years for the first graduates from its proposed undergraduate mining engineering program.\nA planned master’s degree in mining and mineral processing would take 12 to 16 months and help existing employees gain skills sooner, Salim Ahmed, Memorial’s head of process engineering, told The Northern Miner ’s Devan Murugan in an interview this month in St. John’s. The university also plans shorter courses for practising engineers and other workers.\n“It will have flexibility to accommodate the need of the industry,” Ahmed said. “There will be significant demand for skilled manpower. At the same time, we’re not getting enough people, and people are retiring.”\nThe challenge extends beyond replacing retiring workers: graduates will need to handle autonomous equipment, growing volumes of operational data and new maintenance technologies. Memorial aims to integrate those skills throughout its teaching, with an undergraduate program targeting a fall 2027 intake.\nWatch the interview:\nPrint\nREGION: Canada\nRepublish this article",
